{"J1": 2, "J2": 2, "J3": 1, "J4": 2, "J5": 2, "errors_noted": "J1: correctly identifies the 160 rps ceiling of 4x t3.micro vs 750 rps origin peak and the single-NAT dependency, but says nothing about the original's cost weaknesses (e.g. that it already costs little but is under-provisioned, or that CloudFront/NAT drivers change under the new demand) and does not address RPO/RTO gaps in the original. J2: each choice is named with a one-line reason, but no trade-offs are stated (why t3.medium rather than m6g.large; why ElastiCache is worth ~$50/month with no read replica; why asg_max 6 rather than 5; why two NAT gateways rather than accepting the single-NAT risk). Scaling claim 'capacity above baseline is paid for only during the 60 peak hours' is asserted, not reasoned about whether 2 baseline instances (320 rps) suffice for the 150 rps average plus failure headroom. J3: gives only a single total figure and a bare 'within budget' statement; no identification of the main cost drivers (CloudFront GB-out at 2,000 GB is by far the largest line, then RDS Multi-AZ and EC2) and no explanation of why S3 jumps 50->500 GB, RDS storage 20->100 GB, LCU 2->5, or NAT GB 100->200 - these quantities appear in the BOM unjustified, so the narrative is not consistent with the BOM. J5 factual/technical errors: (a) arithmetic/capacity inconsistency - 6 x t3.medium at 160 rps = 960 rps, but the claimed 'headroom for one instance failure' is false because 5 remaining instances give 800 rps only marginally above 750, and with 2 AZs the loss of an entire AZ (claimed in the comparison) leaves 3 instances / 480 rps, so 'tolerates the loss of an Availability Zone' is incorrect; (b) 'the original ... supports only 160 rps at maximum scale and depends on one NAT gateway' is right, but the comparison omits that the original also already has Multi-AZ RDS, so the failover improvement is overstated; (c) point-in-time recovery with 7-day retention does support a 5-minute RPO, but the submission attributes the RPO to 'Multi-AZ ... automated backups and PITR' conflating Multi-AZ synchronous replication (an availability feature) with backup-based recovery; (d) per-AZ NAT gateways improve AZ-fault isolation but are described as removing a 'cross-zone dependency' loosely; (e) ElastiCache is asserted to absorb metadata queries with no evidence of database load being a bottleneck. Style: roughly the final third of the justification is contentless padding that restates that the design is good without adding reasoning, which depresses J2 and J3."}
