{"J1": 2, "J2": 2, "J3": 1, "J4": 2, "J5": 2, "errors_noted": "Capacity claims inconsistent: text says 4 t3.micro give 160 rps (correct) but then claims max 6 t3.medium = 960 rps while also asserting headroom for one instance failure (960 at full fleet leaves only 800 with one lost, still above 750, but this is never reasoned); no sizing argument for the 150 rps average baseline of 2 instances (320 rps, fine but unstated). N3 handled: Multi-AZ plus PITR is correctly described for RPO 5 min / RTO 1 h. Factual slip: Multi-AZ failover is attributed partly to 'automated backups and point-in-time recovery' meeting the RPO, conflating synchronous standby with backup-based recovery. ElastiCache is asserted to improve performance with no analysis of what it caches or whether it was needed for the budget. Claim that 'capacity above the baseline is paid for only during the 60 peak hours' is stated as a cost-optimisation rationale but is not reconciled with the BOM, which also doubles NAT gateways, quintuples S3 storage (50 to 500 GB) and quintuples RDS storage (20 to 100 GB) with no justification for those growth assumptions; LCU rise from 2 to 5 is asserted, not derived. No cost breakdown at all - only the two totals are quoted, so the main cost drivers (CloudFront 2,000 GB, NAT hours, RDS Multi-AZ) are never identified. Justification is heavily padded: roughly the final third is content-free restatement ('robust, scalable, secure and cost-effective', 'a balanced and well-considered response'), displacing the per-pillar evidence the task required. Comparison is one sentence, accurate as far as it goes, but one-sided: it names no trade-off accepted (4.4x cost increase, added NAT and cache spend, operational complexity) and does not acknowledge that the original met its own smaller workload."}
